The question is about PHP. Let me analyze this.

```php
$a = [1, 2];
$b = $a;
$b[] = 3;
echo count($a);
```

In PHP 7+, arrays are passed by value. So `$b = $a` creates a copy of the array. When we modify `$b` with `$b[] = 3`, it doesn't affect `$a`. So `count($a)` returns 2.

This is different from PHP 5.3 and earlier where arrays were passed by reference (copy-on-write). But in PHP 7+, the copy-on-write optimization means `$b = $a` doesn't actually copy until one of them is modified. But even so, modifying `$b` doesn't affect `$a`.

So the answer is 2.

